const policies = [{"id":"POL-001","title":"Terms of Service","category":"Core","version":"0.1-draft","markdown":"# Alpha Terms of Service\n\n**Document ID:** POL-001  \n**Version:** 0.1-draft  \n**Status:** Draft — not approved for publication  \n**Effective date:** Not effective until formally published  \n**Last updated:** 16 November 2026  \n**Operator:** [FULL LEGAL NAME — REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Country of establishment:** The Netherlands\n\n> **Draft notice:** This document is a working draft. It is not yet a binding or approved set of terms. Complete every marked item and obtain an appropriate legal review before presenting it as accepted Terms of Service.\n\n## 1. About these Terms\n\nThese Terms of Service (\"Terms\") are intended to govern access to and use of Alpha services, including Alpha Launcher, Alpha Client, Alpha Accounts, and the community features that Alpha makes available.\n\nThe person operating Alpha is a private individual established in the Netherlands, not a company, based on the operator information currently provided for this draft.\n\n**Operator's legal name:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Contact email:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Postal address for legal notices:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Official service website:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\nDo not treat a display name, username, or project name as a substitute for the operator's legal identity where the law requires the identity of the contracting party.\n\n## 2. Acceptance and eligibility\n\nThese Terms must be made available before a user is asked to accept them. The method of acceptance and the date/version recorded for each acceptance must be confirmed before publication.\n\n**Minimum age and parental-consent requirements:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION AND LEGAL REVIEW]\n\nNothing in these Terms is intended to remove rights that cannot legally be excluded or limited.\n\n## 3. Alpha services\n\nAlpha may provide software, account features, community functionality, game-client modifications, cosmetic listings, and related services. The features available at any given time are those actually released and accessible in the relevant Alpha product.\n\nA description, mock-up, roadmap item, or Store listing does not by itself guarantee that a feature is available.\n\nAlpha may update, maintain, change, suspend, or discontinue parts of the services. Any contractual limits on these powers, notice requirements, and remedies must comply with mandatory applicable law and must be specified before publication.\n\n## 4. Accounts and account security\n\nUsers are responsible for providing accurate information where required and for taking reasonable steps to protect their account credentials and access methods.\n\nUsers must not:\n- use another person's account without permission;\n- impersonate another person or misrepresent their affiliation with Alpha;\n- attempt to bypass authentication, permissions, or access controls;\n- exploit account or service vulnerabilities for unauthorized access.\n\nThe actual account recovery, account deletion, and account-security procedures must be documented before this section is finalized.\n\n**Account recovery and support route:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 5. Acceptable use and community conduct\n\nUsers must comply with the Alpha Acceptable Use Policy and Community Guidelines when those policies apply to the feature being used.\n\nAlpha may investigate reported violations and apply proportionate measures under the published rules. The available measures, evidence-handling process, appeal route, and any emergency exceptions must be defined in the relevant policies before enforcement is promised.\n\n## 6. Ranks, permissions, and staff access\n\nRanks and permissions are governed by the Alpha Ranks and Permissions Policy and by the permissions actually implemented in Alpha.\n\nA rank name or visual badge does not grant permissions beyond those assigned by the applicable access-control system. Alpha's documented rank hierarchy and Founder/Owner access boundaries must be preserved in all related policy text.\n\n## 7. Store and digital content\n\nThe Alpha Store interface may display cosmetic or other digital-content listings. The existence of a listing does not establish that a purchase can be made.\n\n**Before any sale is enabled, Alpha must identify the seller, available products, total prices, payment provider, delivery method, withdrawal information, refund/remedy process, and applicable consumer disclosures.** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION AND LEGAL REVIEW]\n\nNo clause in these Terms waives mandatory consumer rights under applicable Dutch or EU law.\n\n## 8. Third-party services\n\nAlpha may rely on third-party services for functions such as authentication, hosting, or integrations. The actual providers, their roles, and the applicable third-party terms must be identified in the relevant product and privacy documentation.\n\nThird-party services may have their own terms and privacy notices. Alpha must not imply that it controls a third party's service or policies where it does not.\n\n## 9. Suspension and termination\n\nThe grounds, scope, duration, notice, and appeal process for restricting or terminating an account or access to a service must be set out in the applicable moderation and account procedures.\n\nAny suspension or termination must be handled consistently with mandatory law and any rights the user has under a contract for a digital service or digital content.\n\n**Detailed suspension, termination, and appeal procedure:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 10. Software, intellectual property, and licenses\n\nUsers must respect applicable intellectual-property rights and the licenses that apply to Alpha software and third-party components.\n\nThe exact ownership, license grants, permitted redistribution, modding permissions, and notices for Alpha Client and Alpha Launcher must be confirmed against the repositories and included third-party licenses before this section is finalized.\n\nNothing in these Terms transfers ownership of a user's own content to Alpha unless a separate, clearly stated agreement says so.\n\n## 11. Disclaimers and liability\n\nAlpha must not use this section to exclude or limit liability where such exclusion or limitation is prohibited by mandatory law.\n\nAny permitted limitation must be drafted specifically for the actual service and reviewed under Dutch and EU law. No blanket \"as is\" or no-liability wording should be treated as valid merely because it appears in this draft.\n\n**Service-specific warranty and liability wording:** [REQUIRES LEGAL REVIEW]\n\n## 12. Changes to these Terms\n\nEach published version must show its version number, publication date, and effective date. Material changes must be communicated in a way appropriate to the change and in accordance with applicable law.\n\nWhere renewed consent is legally required, continued use alone must not be treated as a substitute for that consent.\n\n## 13. Governing law and disputes\n\nThese Terms are intended to be governed by the laws of the Netherlands, subject to mandatory consumer-protection rules and any mandatory rules that apply because of the user's place of residence.\n\nAny court or dispute-resolution clause must preserve mandatory jurisdiction and consumer rights. A final venue clause requires legal review.\n\n## 14. Contact\n\nQuestions about these Terms can be sent to:\n\n**Operator:** [FULL LEGAL NAME — REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Email:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Postal address:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 15. Related policies\n\n- POL-002 — Privacy Policy\n- POL-003 — Acceptable Use Policy\n- POL-004 — Community Guidelines\n- POL-006 — Ranks and Permissions Policy\n- POL-007 — Store and Digital Content Policy\n- POL-010 — Service Availability and Third-Party Services Policy\n\n---\n\n## Internal completion checklist — remove before publication\n\n- [ ] Confirm operator's full legal name and contact details.\n- [ ] Confirm whether a postal address must be displayed and how it will be provided lawfully.\n- [ ] Confirm age eligibility and parental-consent design.\n- [ ] Confirm the exact services covered and the contracting flow.\n- [ ] Verify account recovery, deletion, suspension, and appeal behavior.\n- [ ] Verify software licensing and third-party notices.\n- [ ] Confirm whether any Store transaction is enabled; if so, identify seller and all consumer disclosures.\n- [ ] Obtain Dutch/EU legal review.\n- [ ] Remove all unresolved markers and this internal checklist before publication.\n"},{"id":"POL-002","title":"Privacy Policy","category":"Privacy","version":"0.3-draft","markdown":"# Alpha Privacy Policy\n\n**Document ID:** POL-002  \n**Version:** 0.3-draft  \n**Status:** Draft — not approved for publication  \n**Effective date:** Not effective until formally published  \n**Last updated:** 16 November 2026  \n**Controller:** [FULL LEGAL NAME — REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Country of establishment:** The Netherlands\n\n> **Draft notice:** This notice includes a review of the live Alpha Supabase database schema, its current tables, selected database policies, authentication metadata structure, storage configuration, and deployed Edge Function code on 16 November 2026. It is not a complete audit of every application, network, provider, or production data flow. Do not publish it until the remaining items are verified and the notice has received appropriate legal review.\n\n## 1. Who is responsible for your personal data?\n\nAlpha is currently operated by a private individual established in the Netherlands. The data controller must be identified by the operator's full legal name.\n\n**Data controller:** [FULL LEGAL NAME — REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Privacy contact email:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Postal address:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\nFor the purposes of the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR/AVG), the controller is the person who determines the purposes and means of processing personal data. The exact controller identity and any joint-controller arrangements must be confirmed before publication.\n\n## 2. Which services does this notice cover?\n\nThis notice is intended to cover Alpha services that the controller operates, including, where enabled:\n\n- Alpha Launcher and Alpha Client account-related functionality;\n- Alpha Accounts and community profiles;\n- friends, online presence, and private messaging;\n- links between Alpha profiles and Minecraft accounts;\n- rank, permission, staff, and moderation systems;\n- Pixel Garden account saves and progression;\n- support, security, and administrative processes;\n- release-management functions used by authorized Alpha staff.\n\nThe live Supabase project inspected for this draft is named **AlphaClient** and reports its database region as **EU West (Ireland / eu-west-1)**. This is the configured project region returned by Supabase; it does not by itself identify every subprocessor, backup location, transfer, or legal entity involved in processing.\n\n## 3. What information Alpha receives and stores\n\nThe following inventory is based on the Supabase database schema and deployed function code inspected on 16 November 2026. It describes the information represented by the current database structures. Some fields are populated only when a feature is used, and some records are created by Alpha or automatically by the service rather than typed directly by a user.\n\n### 3.1 Information you provide when creating and using an Alpha Account\n\n| Information | What Alpha's Supabase setup contains | How it is obtained |\n|---|---|---|\n| Email address | An email field in the authentication user record and linked identity record | Supplied during email-based account registration or account management |\n| Password credential | Supabase Auth maintains an authentication password-credential field; the Alpha profile table does not contain a plaintext password field | Submitted to Supabase Auth for authentication; Alpha's application should not ask for or store your plaintext password separately |\n| Alpha username | A username field in the community profile and optional `username` authentication metadata | Supplied during registration or profile setup; a database trigger uses the supplied username metadata when creating the profile |\n| Display name | A display-name field in the community profile and optional `display_name` authentication metadata | Supplied during registration or profile editing |\n| Profile biography | A `bio` field in the community profile | Supplied by the user if the profile biography feature is used |\n| Profile avatar | An `avatar_url` field and an uploaded image object in the `profile-avatars` storage bucket | Uploaded by the user if an avatar is selected |\n| Minecraft account identifier | A Minecraft UUID in the profile/link tables | Created when a Minecraft account is linked to an Alpha profile |\n\nThe current authentication identity provider recorded in the inspected database is email. The database also has phone-related authentication columns, but no phone numbers were populated in the current user records inspected for this draft. This is a current database observation, not a promise that phone authentication can never be introduced.\n\nSupabase Auth also maintains internal account and security records, including account IDs, identity-provider metadata, email-confirmation status and timestamps, account creation/update timestamps, last-sign-in timestamps, authentication sessions, refresh-token records, and fields used for confirmation, recovery, reauthentication, and email changes. These are authentication-system records; they are not all fields that a user manually enters into an Alpha profile.\n\nThe authentication schema contains a password-credential field named `encrypted_password`. Alpha's public profile table does not contain a plaintext password field. The exact credential handling and security controls are also governed by Supabase Auth and its applicable documentation.\n\n### 3.2 Profile and community information\n\nThe Supabase `profiles` table contains:\n\n- Alpha account/profile ID;\n- Alpha username;\n- display name;\n- biography;\n- avatar URL;\n- community rank key;\n- linked Minecraft UUID;\n- profile creation and update timestamps.\n\nSome profile attributes, such as the community rank, are controlled by Alpha's server-side permission and rank systems rather than freely supplied by the user.\n\nAuthenticated Alpha users can read community profiles under the current database policy. The profile-avatar storage bucket is configured as public, meaning avatar files in that bucket are publicly readable. The current bucket configuration allows PNG, JPEG, WebP, and GIF files, with a maximum file size of 5 MB. Users can upload, update, and delete their own avatar files under the configured storage policies.\n\n### 3.3 Friends, messages, and community activity\n\nWhen these community features are used, Supabase stores:\n\n- friend-request sender and recipient account IDs;\n- friend-request status and creation/response timestamps;\n- friendship records identifying the two accounts;\n- private-message sender and recipient account IDs;\n- the text/body of each private message;\n- private-message creation timestamps;\n- profile activity, including the last-played timestamp and last-played AlphaClient version.\n\nThe current database policy allows only the sender and recipient to read a private-message record. Sending a private message is restricted to accounts that are friends under the current database policy. Friend-request records are readable by their sender and recipient.\n\n### 3.4 Minecraft account links and active sessions\n\nWhen a user links a Minecraft account, the database stores:\n\n- the Alpha profile ID;\n- the Minecraft UUID;\n- the time the link was created.\n\nThe active Minecraft session table also contains a session ID, Alpha profile ID, Minecraft UUID, heartbeat timestamp, and session-expiry timestamp. These fields support the current session/presence-related functionality.\n\nNo Minecraft password field was found in the inspected Supabase application tables. This does not establish how every external Minecraft or Microsoft authentication component handles credentials; that separate authentication flow must be audited before publication.\n\n### 3.5 Online presence and staff presence\n\nThe staff-presence table contains:\n\n- the staff user's account ID and Alpha name;\n- online and AFK status;\n- last-seen and last-heartbeat timestamps;\n- session-start and record-update timestamps.\n\nThe current database policies restrict this staff-presence information to staff access. This section describes the staff-presence table; it does not establish that Alpha records a complete activity history for every ordinary user.\n\n### 3.6 Pixel Garden game progress\n\nWhen Pixel Garden is used while signed in, Supabase stores a save associated with the user's account ID. The save data currently has fields for game state such as:\n\n- inventory and seeds;\n- plots and unlocked plots;\n- growth levels and progression;\n- total harvested amounts;\n- purchased upgrades and upgrade coins;\n- automatic harvest, planting, selling, quest, and crafting settings;\n- crafting inventory, active orders, queued orders, ready orders, and capacity/queue levels;\n- reset-token and save-processing state.\n\nThe related Pixel Garden prestige table stores account-linked game progression, including prestige coins, active seconds, heartbeat time, prestige count, upgrade steps, multiplier override, upgrade settings, and update time. A separate reset-token record stores the account's token count and update time.\n\nThese are game-state and gameplay-progress records associated with an Alpha account. They are not a record of the user's real-world financial balance.\n\nThe current database policy for Pixel Garden saves allows a signed-in user to read and update their own save. The full behavior of every game event and every client-side calculation has not been independently audited for this notice.\n\n### 3.7 Ranks, permissions, and account verification\n\nThe database also stores information used to administer Alpha accounts and access, including:\n\n- rank definitions and rank display metadata;\n- rank assignments, assignment timestamps, and the account that made an assignment;\n- rank permission settings and the account that last changed them;\n- per-user module/category grants, effects, reasons, expiry times, revocation times, and the staff account responsible;\n- per-user command permission overrides and update information;\n- community verification status, verifier account ID, and verification timestamps;\n\nThese records are generally created or changed by authorized Alpha staff or server-side processes. They are not all information submitted by the affected user.\n\n### 3.8 Staff operations, notes, and administrative records\n\nFor staff and moderation functionality, the database contains structures for:\n\n- staff operation records, including record type, title, status, creator, assignee, timestamps, and a JSON payload;\n- staff module activity, including actor account ID/name, module, event type, action, target label, details, and event timestamp;\n- staff player notes, including player name, note text, author account ID/name, and creation time;\n- staff private notes, including subject name, note text, author account ID/name, and creation/update times;\n- command-permission notes, including permission identifier, note text, author account ID, and creation time.\n\nDepending on the feature, note text or record details may contain information about a user or a Minecraft player. Staff-only access rules apply to several of these records. The precise access and retention rules for each staff record type still need a separate review.\n\n### 3.9 Security and administrative audit records\n\nThe Supabase `audit_logs` table can record:\n\n- actor account ID and actor label;\n- target account ID and target label, where relevant;\n- product and category;\n- action performed;\n- outcome, such as success, failure, or denied;\n- structured details, which may include before/after values, rank or permission identifiers, Alpha names, item/quantity information, or other action-specific details;\n- request ID and event timestamp.\n\nThe deployed permission, Easter Egg, and release-management functions also write administrative activity to audit records for relevant actions. These records are not a general transcript of everything a user does in Alpha; they document the administrative actions and events that the relevant code sends to the audit system.\n\nThe Supabase Auth schema includes an audit-log table with an IP-address field. That table contained no rows at the time of this inspection. Whether IP addresses are populated or retained by the live authentication configuration, infrastructure logs, or other providers has not been established. Alpha must verify this before stating that IP addresses are or are not collected.\n\n### 3.10 Alpha avatar storage\n\nThe inspected Supabase project has a `profile-avatars` storage bucket configured as public. Its current configuration allows PNG, JPEG, WebP, and GIF files and sets a 5 MB file-size limit. The storage policies allow public reading and restrict authenticated uploads, updates, and deletions to the user's own account folder.\n\nUsers should not upload an avatar that contains personal information they do not want to make publicly accessible.\n\n## 4. Information not established as collected by the inspected Supabase schema\n\nThe following distinctions are important:\n\n- **Microsoft account passwords and access tokens:** No Microsoft password or Microsoft OAuth-token columns were found in the inspected Supabase application tables. The Launcher source previously inspected stores saved Microsoft-account display information locally. That local Launcher storage is separate from the Supabase database. The complete Microsoft sign-in and token flow has not been audited here.\n- **Phone numbers:** Phone-related columns exist in Supabase Auth, but no phone numbers were populated in the current user records inspected.\n- **Payment-card or transaction details:** No payment-card or payment-transaction tables were found in the inspected Supabase application schema. A Store catalogue or cosmetic listing does not prove that checkout or payment processing is active. Any external payment provider or future payment flow must be documented separately before it is enabled.\n- **Precise device location:** No precise-location field was identified in the inspected application tables.\n- **Advertising identifiers or analytics profiles:** No such fields were identified in the inspected application tables. The full client telemetry, crash-reporting, and third-party SDK configuration has not been audited.\n- **Minecraft passwords:** No Minecraft password field was identified in the inspected Supabase application tables.\n\nThe absence of a field from the inspected database does not prove that no other component, local application, external provider, server log, or future feature processes that information. These areas remain subject to further verification.\n\n## 5. Information processed locally by the Launcher\n\nThe Launcher source inspected separately contains local storage for launcher settings and saved Microsoft-account display information. This is not the same as information stored in the Alpha Supabase project.\n\nThe complete local-storage inventory, operating-system protection, account-token handling, and any information transmitted from the Launcher to other providers must be documented in a separate end-to-end data-flow review. Do not assume that every locally stored item is sent to Alpha.\n\n## 6. Why personal data is processed\n\nThe following are the purposes suggested by the inspected features. The controller must confirm each purpose and select a valid GDPR legal basis for each processing activity before publication.\n\n| Potential purpose | Related information | Legal basis |\n|---|---|---|\n| Create and authenticate Alpha Accounts | Email, account ID, authentication records | [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION] |\n| Create and maintain community profiles | Username, display name, biography, avatar, profile timestamps | [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION] |\n| Provide friends and private messaging | Friend requests, friendship links, message content and timestamps | [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION] |\n| Link Minecraft accounts and support session features | Minecraft UUID, link timestamp, session/heartbeat timestamps | [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION] |\n| Provide profile and staff presence features | Online/AFK status and presence timestamps | [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION] |\n| Save Pixel Garden progress | Inventory, plots, upgrades, progression, gameplay timers and related save data | [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION] |\n| Administer ranks, permissions, and verification | Rank assignments, permission grants, verification and staff actor records | [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION] |\n| Maintain moderation, support, and administrative records | Notes, staff records, audit events and action details | [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION] |\n| Maintain security and investigate misuse | Authentication records and relevant audit/security events | [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION] |\n\nA legal basis must be determined separately for each purpose. Alpha must not use consent as a catch-all basis or claim legitimate interests without documenting the assessment.\n\n## 7. Where data is stored and who receives it\n\nThe inspected Alpha Supabase project is hosted in the **EU West (Ireland / eu-west-1)** region. The project uses Supabase Auth, Supabase Database, Supabase Storage, and deployed Supabase Edge Functions.\n\n**Supabase contracting entity and applicable data-processing agreement:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Other hosting, infrastructure, or service providers:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Other recipients or subprocessors:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**International transfers and safeguards, if any:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\nThe final notice must identify recipients or categories of recipients and explain any transfers outside the European Economic Area, including the safeguards relied upon where required.\n\n## 8. How long data is kept\n\nThe schema contains creation, update, activity, expiry, and session timestamps for various records, but a timestamp or expiry field is not a complete retention policy. No verified retention schedule was established by this inspection.\n\n**Retention periods or objective retention criteria for each data category:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\nAlpha must define retention for account records, profile data, avatars, friend requests, private messages, Minecraft links and sessions, presence records, Pixel Garden saves, staff notes, audit logs, security records, backups, and any Store records that actually exist. Data must not be described as deleted immediately unless the full deletion and backup lifecycle supports that statement.\n\n## 9. Your privacy rights\n\nDepending on the circumstances and applicable law, you may have the right to:\n\n- receive information about the processing of your personal data;\n- request access to your personal data;\n- request correction of inaccurate or incomplete data;\n- request erasure of personal data;\n- request restriction of processing;\n- object to certain processing;\n- receive or transfer certain data in a portable format;\n- withdraw consent where processing is based on consent, without affecting processing that took place before withdrawal;\n- lodge a complaint with the Dutch Data Protection Authority (Autoriteit Persoonsgegevens).\n\nThese rights are subject to the conditions and exceptions in the GDPR. The right to erasure, for example, is not absolute.\n\n## 10. How to make a privacy request\n\nSend privacy requests to:\n\n**Email:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\nAlpha must implement a process to verify requests proportionately, respond within the applicable legal time limits, and protect the rights of other people whose data may appear in the requested records.\n\n**Request procedure and identity-verification method:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 11. Children and age-related processing\n\n**Minimum age for Alpha services:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Parental-consent process, if applicable:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\nDo not publish an age threshold or claim that parental consent is collected until the actual product flow and legal requirements have been checked.\n\n## 12. Security\n\nAlpha should use appropriate technical and organisational measures to protect personal data. The database has row-level security enabled on the inspected application tables and contains access policies for several features. This inspection was not a complete security assessment and does not establish that every table, function, storage object, or production configuration is correctly protected.\n\n**Verified security measures and incident contact route:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\nNo statement in this draft guarantees that a service is completely secure.\n\n## 13. Cookies, local storage, telemetry, and similar technologies\n\nThe Launcher source inspected separately uses local storage for certain launcher settings and saved Microsoft-account display information. This Supabase review does not establish whether Alpha websites or applications use cookies, analytics, telemetry, crash reporting, advertising identifiers, or other tracking technologies.\n\n**Complete cookie/local-storage/telemetry inventory and consent requirements:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\nIf separate cookie or tracking information is required, it will be provided in POL-011.\n\n## 14. Changes to this Privacy Policy\n\nThe published policy will display its version and effective date. If the purposes or manner of processing change, Alpha will update this notice and provide any additional information or obtain any consent required by law.\n\n## 15. Complaints\n\nYou may contact Alpha using the privacy contact details above. You also have the right to lodge a complaint with the Dutch Data Protection Authority:\n\n**Autoriteit Persoonsgegevens:** https://autoriteitpersoonsgegevens.nl/\n\n## 16. Contact\n\n**Data controller:** [FULL LEGAL NAME — REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Privacy email:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Postal address:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Country:** The Netherlands\n\n---\n\n## Internal completion checklist — remove before publication\n\n- [ ] Insert controller's full legal identity and valid contact details.\n- [ ] Confirm the Supabase contracting entity, data-processing agreement, subprocessors, and hosting/backup locations.\n- [ ] Complete an end-to-end data inventory covering Launcher, Client, Supabase, Microsoft authentication, third-party SDKs, telemetry, crash reporting, logs, and support.\n- [ ] Confirm whether authentication or infrastructure logs collect IP addresses and define their retention.\n- [ ] Determine and document a valid legal basis for every processing purpose.\n- [ ] Set and implement retention periods, including backups and deletion behavior.\n- [ ] Verify avatar visibility and explain the public bucket clearly in the user interface.\n- [ ] Verify user rights workflows and response ownership.\n- [ ] Confirm age policy and any parental-consent mechanism.\n- [ ] Verify security measures and incident-response procedures.\n- [ ] Audit cookies, local storage, analytics, telemetry, and crash reporting.\n- [ ] Confirm whether the Store accepts payments; if so, document the seller, payment provider, data received, and consumer disclosures.\n- [ ] Review whether a record of processing activities, DPIA, or other GDPR measures are required.\n- [ ] Obtain Dutch/EU data-protection legal review.\n- [ ] Remove all unresolved markers and this internal checklist before publication.\n"},{"id":"POL-003","title":"Acceptable Use Policy","category":"Safety","version":"0.1-draft","markdown":"# Alpha Acceptable Use Policy\n\n**Document ID:** POL-003  \n**Version:** 0.1-draft  \n**Status:** Draft — not approved for publication  \n**Effective date:** Not effective until formally published  \n**Last updated:** 16 November 2026  \n**Operator:** [FULL LEGAL NAME — REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n> **Draft notice:** This policy is a working draft. It must be checked against the released product, the Terms of Service, and applicable Dutch/EU law before publication or enforcement.\n\n## 1. Purpose\n\nThis policy sets baseline rules for safe, lawful, and fair use of Alpha services. It applies alongside POL-001 (Terms of Service), POL-004 (Community Guidelines), and any feature-specific rules shown in Alpha.\n\n## 2. Scope\n\nThis policy is intended to cover Alpha Launcher, Alpha Client, Alpha Accounts, and Alpha-operated community features made available to the user. It applies when using an Alpha account, connecting to Alpha services, communicating through Alpha features, or interacting with Alpha staff.\n\nA rule in this policy applies only to a feature that Alpha actually operates or makes available. It does not make Alpha responsible for independent Minecraft servers, third-party platforms, or services outside Alpha's control.\n\n## 3. Account and access security\n\nYou must:\n- use only accounts you own or are explicitly authorized to use;\n- keep your sign-in methods and recovery methods reasonably secure;\n- provide accurate information where Alpha requires it;\n- report suspected account compromise or unauthorized access;\n- use ranks and permissions only within the access granted to your account.\n\nYou must not:\n- impersonate another user, staff member, or Alpha representative;\n- share, sell, rent, or transfer account access where this creates unauthorized access or violates applicable account rules;\n- bypass authentication, verification, rank checks, rate limits, or other access controls;\n- access another user's private messages, profile controls, saves, or staff information without authorization;\n- use automation or scripts to probe or overload Alpha services without written permission.\n\n## 4. Prohibited conduct\n\nYou must not use Alpha services to:\n- threaten, harass, stalk, intimidate, or target another person;\n- publish another person's private or identifying information without a lawful basis and appropriate authorization;\n- send spam, scams, phishing messages, malicious links, or deceptive promotions;\n- distribute malware or attempt to compromise a user's device, account, or Alpha infrastructure;\n- exploit bugs to obtain unauthorized access, items, ranks, permissions, data, or other advantages;\n- interfere with service availability, integrity, or other users' ability to use Alpha;\n- violate applicable law or infringe intellectual-property or privacy rights;\n- evade a valid restriction by creating or using another account.\n\n## 5. Software, game clients, and third-party environments\n\nUse Alpha Client and Launcher in accordance with their displayed functionality, license terms, and the rules of the server or platform on which you choose to use them.\n\nAlpha does not set the rules of independent Minecraft servers. A feature being present in Alpha Client does not mean that a third-party server permits it. You are responsible for checking and following the rules of the environment in which you play.\n\nDo not misrepresent Alpha's relationship with Mojang, Microsoft, a server operator, or another third party.\n\n## 6. Security research and vulnerability testing\n\nDo not test, scan, exploit, or attempt to gain access to systems you do not own or have permission to test. Good-faith reports about suspected vulnerabilities should be sent through the official reporting route once Alpha publishes one.\n\n**Security reporting contact and authorized testing scope:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION — see POL-009]\n\n## 7. Enforcement\n\nDepending on the facts, severity, risk, and applicable law, Alpha may review a report and consider measures such as:\n- a warning or request to stop the conduct;\n- removal or restriction of offending content where Alpha controls it;\n- temporary restriction of a relevant feature or permission;\n- temporary account or service restriction;\n- permanent restriction or account termination in serious or repeated cases.\n\nThese are possible measures, not a claim that every measure is currently implemented. The actual decision-maker, evidence process, notice, duration, and appeal route must be confirmed and published before this section is used as an enforcement promise.\n\n**Enforcement and appeal procedure:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 8. Reporting a concern\n\nProvide the relevant account name, feature, approximate date/time, and a concise description. Include only evidence that you are entitled to share. Do not post private messages, personal data, or security-sensitive details publicly.\n\n**Official support/reporting route:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 9. Changes\n\nAlpha will identify the version and effective date of each published revision. Material changes will be communicated as required by applicable law and the Terms of Service.\n\n## 10. Related policies\n\n- POL-001 — Terms of Service\n- POL-002 — Privacy Policy\n- POL-004 — Community Guidelines\n- POL-005 — Staff Code of Conduct\n- POL-009 — Security and Vulnerability Disclosure Policy\n\n## Internal completion checklist — remove before publication\n\n- [ ] Confirm covered services and the exact enforcement powers available.\n- [ ] Confirm report intake, evidence handling, decision ownership, and appeal route.\n- [ ] Confirm whether automation/rate limits are technically enforced.\n- [ ] Add the official security and support contacts.\n- [ ] Obtain legal review and align with POL-001 and POL-004.\n- [ ] Remove unresolved markers and this checklist before publication.\n"},{"id":"POL-004","title":"Community Guidelines","category":"Community","version":"0.1-draft","markdown":"# Alpha Community Guidelines\n\n**Document ID:** POL-004  \n**Version:** 0.1-draft  \n**Status:** Draft — not approved for publication  \n**Effective date:** Not effective until formally published  \n**Last updated:** 16 November 2026  \n**Operator:** [FULL LEGAL NAME — REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n> **Draft notice:** These guidelines are a working draft for Alpha-operated community spaces and features. They do not replace the rules of independent Minecraft servers or third-party platforms.\n\n## 1. Purpose and scope\n\nThese guidelines describe the expected standard of conduct when using Alpha community features, including profiles, friend requests, private messages, presence features, and any Alpha-operated community space that is formally brought within scope.\n\nThey apply to users regardless of rank. A rank, badge, staff role, or account status does not exempt anyone from these guidelines.\n\n## 2. Treat people respectfully\n\nYou must not:\n- harass, threaten, bully, or repeatedly target another person;\n- use discriminatory slurs or attack people based on protected or personal characteristics;\n- encourage violence or credible threats;\n- coordinate harassment or encourage others to target a user;\n- continue unwanted contact after a clear request to stop, subject to legitimate support or moderation communication.\n\nDisagreement, criticism, and good-faith debate are allowed when expressed without abuse or targeted harassment.\n\n## 3. Profiles and user-generated content\n\nWhen using a profile, avatar, biography, or other user-submitted content:\n- use content you have the right to use;\n- do not impersonate another person or an official Alpha account;\n- do not publish private information about another person without authorization;\n- do not use content that is threatening, harassing, unlawful, or designed to deceive;\n- remember that profile information and avatar visibility may be broader than a private conversation.\n\nThe current Supabase configuration inspected for POL-002 makes profile avatars publicly readable. Do not upload an image that you do not want to be publicly accessible.\n\n## 4. Friends and private messages\n\nUse friend requests and private messages for genuine communication. Do not use them for spam, scams, phishing, threats, harassment, or unsolicited mass promotion.\n\nDo not assume that a private message is an end-to-end encrypted communication. Do not share passwords, authentication codes, payment credentials, or sensitive personal information in messages.\n\nThe current database policy restricts private-message reads to the sender and recipient and restricts sending to friends. This describes the inspected database policy, not a guarantee against device compromise, account compromise, legal disclosure, or every possible operational access path. See POL-002.\n\n## 5. Names, ranks, and representation\n\nDo not falsely claim to be Founder, Owner, staff, a partner, a verified person, or an official representative. Do not use another person's badge, identity, or account to mislead users.\n\nRank names and permissions are governed by POL-006. Community status does not authorize staff actions.\n\n## 6. Advertising and promotion\n\nDo not flood community features with repetitive promotions, referral links, unsolicited commercial messages, or misleading offers. Any official partnership or promotion must be clearly authorized by Alpha.\n\n**Rules for approved promotions and partner content:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 7. Reporting and moderation\n\nIf you experience or observe a violation, use the official reporting route. Include relevant context and avoid publicly reposting private conversations or personal information.\n\nAlpha may review reports and take proportionate action under POL-003 and POL-005. No particular moderation workflow, response time, or appeal outcome is promised until confirmed.\n\n**Report route, response expectations, and appeal process:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 8. No retaliation\n\nDo not threaten, harass, expose, or punish someone for making a good-faith report, participating in a review, or raising a concern. Knowingly false reports or deliberate evidence manipulation may themselves be reviewed.\n\n## 9. Changes\n\nEach published revision will show its version and effective date. Material changes will be communicated as required by applicable law.\n\n## 10. Related policies\n\n- POL-001 — Terms of Service\n- POL-002 — Privacy Policy\n- POL-003 — Acceptable Use Policy\n- POL-005 — Staff Code of Conduct\n- POL-006 — Ranks and Permissions Policy\n\n## Internal completion checklist — remove before publication\n\n- [ ] Define which Alpha-operated community spaces are covered.\n- [ ] Confirm the reporting, moderation, evidence, and appeal workflows.\n- [ ] Confirm promotion/partner-content rules.\n- [ ] Align sanctions with POL-003 and POL-005.\n- [ ] Obtain legal review and remove unresolved markers/checklist.\n"},{"id":"POL-006","title":"Ranks and Permissions Policy","category":"Access","version":"0.1-draft","markdown":"# Alpha Ranks and Permissions Policy\n\n**Document ID:** POL-006  \n**Version:** 0.1-draft  \n**Status:** Draft — not approved for publication  \n**Effective date:** Not effective until formally published  \n**Last updated:** 16 November 2026  \n**Policy owner:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n> **Draft notice:** This document records Alpha's supplied rank and access requirements. It is not proof that every permission is currently implemented correctly. The live configuration and code must be verified before publication.\n\n## 1. Purpose and scope\n\nThis policy explains the distinction between community ranks, staff ranks, and feature permissions in Alpha. It applies to Alpha profiles, rank badges, StaffMods, and any Alpha service that displays or enforces rank-based access.\n\n## 2. Rank hierarchy\n\nThe project-defined order from lowest to highest is:\n\n1. Member\n2. Scout\n3. Rogue\n4. Elite\n5. Alpha\n6. Alpha+\n7. Beta\n8. The Nibbler\n9. Media\n10. Famous\n11. Partner\n12. Trial Moderator (T-Mod)\n13. Moderator\n14. Sr Moderator\n15. Admin\n16. Sr Admin\n17. Platform Admin\n18. Manager\n19. Developer\n20. Owner\n21. Founder\n\nThe first group through Partner consists of community/donator or special community ranks, not staff ranks. Staff ranks begin at Trial Moderator (T-Mod).\n\n**Beta requirements:** The rank is a beta-tester rank, must be labelled **BETA**, sits directly below The Nibbler in the hierarchy, and must remain separately assignable. It must not be silently merged with another rank.\n\n## 3. Founder and Owner restrictions\n\nFounder is exclusive to the project owner. It is not an ordinary rank that staff can assign to users.\n\nOwner must not receive access to the private Founder folder. Owner access may include the other staff areas allowed by the configured hierarchy, but the Founder-only folder remains excluded.\n\nThese restrictions apply to both user-interface visibility and server-side authorization. Hiding a menu item alone is not sufficient access control.\n\n## 4. Highest-rank display and combined permissions\n\nWhere Alpha displays one primary community/staff rank, it must display only the highest applicable rank. The display label should be **Alpha Rank: [rank]** where that profile presentation is used.\n\nIf a user has multiple assigned ranks, permissions from those assignments may combine according to the permission model. Combining permissions must not cause the interface to display multiple primary ranks where the single-highest-rank display rule applies.\n\nThe community profile rank and staff authorization are distinct concepts. A badge or displayed rank must not be treated as proof of every permission.\n\n## 5. StaffMods access\n\nStaffMods is a restricted staff area, not a public category. Access is limited to eligible staff ranks or explicit staff permissions.\n\nThe project-defined staff hierarchy is:\n- Trial Moderator (T-Mod)\n- Moderator\n- Sr Moderator\n- Admin\n- Sr Admin\n- Platform Admin\n- Manager\n- Developer\n- Owner\n- Founder\n\nGeneral staff modules must be granted according to the configured permission model. Manager and Platform Admin scopes must not be conflated; each retains its defined access boundary.\n\n**Bhop is permitted only in StaffMods → General.** It must be visible/configurable only when the account has the required Bhop permission. It must not appear as a public Movement module or grant an Active Alpha/Bhop HUD to users without the required permission.\n\n## 6. Rank assignment and permission administration\n\nRank assignments and individual permission grants must be made only by authorized staff or trusted server-side processes. Each assignment or change should have an accountable actor, timestamp, and reason where supported by the system.\n\nUsers must not be able to self-assign privileged ranks, Founder status, or staff permissions by editing profile fields or client-side data.\n\n**Exact rank-management roles, approval rules, and appeal route:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 7. Rank benefits and availability\n\nA rank label does not by itself promise a particular cosmetic, module, service, or benefit. Benefits must be documented separately and checked against the current implementation.\n\nAlpha must not advertise a rank benefit as available until it has been verified in the released product.\n\n## 8. Errors, revocation, and disputes\n\nIf a rank or permission appears incorrect, users should report the account name, displayed rank, affected feature, and relevant context through the official support route.\n\nAlpha may correct an erroneous assignment or permission where authorized. Any review or appeal process must be defined before being promised.\n\n**Support and rank-dispute procedure:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 9. Changes\n\nChanges to rank order, rank meaning, Founder exclusivity, Owner boundaries, or staff access are controlled policy changes. They must be approved by the policy owner and reflected consistently in the application, backend authorization, and this document.\n\n## 10. Related policies\n\n- POL-001 — Terms of Service\n- POL-003 — Acceptable Use Policy\n- POL-005 — Staff Code of Conduct\n- POL-002 — Privacy Policy\n\n## Internal verification checklist — remove before publication\n\n- [ ] Compare the full hierarchy with current rank definitions and UI labels.\n- [ ] Verify Beta remains assignable, labelled BETA, and ordered directly below The Nibbler.\n- [ ] Verify Founder exclusivity in server-side logic and assignment tools.\n- [ ] Verify Owner cannot access the Founder folder through UI, API, storage, or direct requests.\n- [ ] Verify only the highest rank is displayed where required while permissions combine as specified.\n- [ ] Verify StaffMods eligibility and Manager/Platform Admin boundaries.\n- [ ] Verify Bhop exists only in StaffMods → General and is permission-gated.\n- [ ] Verify rank assignment audit and appeal procedures.\n- [ ] Remove unresolved markers and obtain owner/legal approval before publication.\n"},{"id":"POL-007","title":"Store and Digital Content Policy","category":"Store","version":"0.1-draft","markdown":"# Alpha Store and Digital Content Policy\n\n**Document ID:** POL-007  \n**Version:** 0.1-draft  \n**Status:** Draft — not approved for publication or sales  \n**Effective date:** Not effective until formally published  \n**Last updated:** 16 November 2026  \n**Seller/operator:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n> **Draft notice:** The inspected Launcher source contains a Store interface and cosmetic catalogue. The inspected application schema did not establish an active checkout or payment system. This draft therefore does not represent that Alpha currently sells digital content.\n\n## 1. Scope\n\nThis policy is intended to apply to cosmetic listings and digital content offered through an Alpha-operated Store, once the seller, purchase flow, delivery, and consumer terms have been confirmed.\n\nA catalogue entry, preview, price label, or planned item is not by itself an offer that can be purchased.\n\n## 2. Current verified position\n\nThe Launcher source inspected for the policy work contains a cosmetic catalogue and Store interface. The inspected Supabase application schema did not contain payment-card or payment-transaction tables. These observations do not rule out an external provider or a separate payment flow; none has been verified.\n\n**Current checkout availability:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Legal seller:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Payment provider and payment methods:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\nUntil these facts are verified, Alpha must not present this draft as purchase terms or claim that an order, payment, refund, or delivery process is active.\n\n## 3. Listings and descriptions\n\nBefore a digital item is offered for sale, its listing must clearly state:\n- the item's name and material characteristics;\n- the price and currency, including mandatory charges;\n- any technical requirements or compatibility limits;\n- whether the item is permanent, time-limited, consumable, or conditional;\n- how and when it is delivered or made available;\n- any restrictions on use, transfer, resale, or account eligibility;\n- the identity of the seller and a usable contact route.\n\nDo not describe a cosmetic as owned, delivered, or guaranteed merely because it appears in a catalogue or preview.\n\n## 4. Orders and payment\n\nNo purchase process is defined by this draft.\n\nBefore enabling checkout, Alpha must document the seller, order confirmation, total price, payment provider, payment data received by Alpha, payment authorization, failed payments, duplicate payments, receipts, order records, and support process.\n\n**Payment and order procedure:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION AND LEGAL REVIEW]\n\nAlpha should not collect or store full payment-card credentials unless a separately reviewed and lawful payment architecture explicitly requires it. Any provider handling payment information must be identified in the Privacy Policy.\n\n## 5. Digital delivery and account access\n\nBefore sale, Alpha must explain when a purchase is considered delivered, which account receives the content, how users access it, and what happens if delivery fails or the account is inaccessible.\n\n**Delivery and entitlement rules:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 6. Withdrawal, refunds, and remedies\n\nMandatory Dutch and EU consumer rights may apply to digital content and digital services. The availability and timing of any withdrawal right, and any exception for immediate digital delivery, depend on the actual contract flow and the legally required information and express consent/acknowledgement.\n\nThis draft does not waive statutory rights and does not promise a blanket no-refund rule.\n\nBefore sales begin, Alpha must provide legally reviewed information about:\n- the right of withdrawal and any applicable exception;\n- required express consent and acknowledgement for immediate digital supply, where relevant;\n- refunds for failed, defective, misdescribed, or non-conforming digital content;\n- statutory conformity remedies and support;\n- complaint handling and response route.\n\n**Final withdrawal/refund/remedy terms:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION AND LEGAL REVIEW]\n\n## 7. Promotions, discounts, and bundles\n\nAny promotion must state its conditions, duration, eligibility, and relevant price information clearly. Alpha must not use misleading reference prices, scarcity claims, or undisclosed restrictions.\n\n**Promotion approval and pricing rules:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 8. Licences and permitted use\n\nA digital item should be used only with the Alpha account and within the scope stated in its listing and applicable licence. Ownership of a digital entitlement does not transfer copyright or other intellectual-property rights in the underlying artwork, software, or design.\n\n**Item licence and transferability:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 9. Account restrictions and purchased content\n\nBefore sales begin, Alpha must explain what happens to paid entitlements if an account is suspended, deleted, compromised, or inaccessible, subject to mandatory consumer law. A moderation action must not be used to remove statutory consumer remedies.\n\n**Purchased-content treatment after account action:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION AND LEGAL REVIEW]\n\n## 10. Third-party platforms\n\nIf a third party processes payments, distributes content, or hosts a Store component, Alpha must identify that party's role and explain which terms apply. Do not imply that a third party endorses Alpha unless that relationship is verified.\n\n## 11. Changes and discontinuation\n\nBefore offering content, Alpha must define how it will notify users about material changes, item removal, service discontinuation, and any effect on existing entitlements. Mandatory consumer rights remain unaffected.\n\n## 12. Contact and related policies\n\n**Seller legal identity:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Store support email:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\nRelated policies:\n- POL-001 — Terms of Service\n- POL-002 — Privacy Policy\n- POL-010 — Service Availability and Third-Party Services Policy\n\n## Internal launch checklist — do not publish as purchase terms until complete\n\n- [ ] Confirm whether checkout is live or planned only.\n- [ ] Identify seller, business/private-individual status for the sale, address, and contact details.\n- [ ] Identify payment provider, payment methods, fees, taxes, and data flows.\n- [ ] Define order confirmation, receipts, failed-payment handling, and delivery.\n- [ ] Obtain Dutch/EU consumer-law review of withdrawal, digital-content consent, conformity, and remedies.\n- [ ] Define licences, transfers, account restrictions, and discontinuation handling.\n- [ ] Update POL-002 for payment and order data actually processed.\n- [ ] Remove unresolved markers and obtain approval before enabling sales.\n"},{"id":"POL-008","title":"Beta Testing Policy","category":"Programs","version":"0.1-draft","markdown":"# Alpha Beta Testing Policy\n\n**Document ID:** POL-008  \n**Version:** 0.1-draft  \n**Status:** Draft — not approved for publication  \n**Effective date:** Not effective until formally published  \n**Last updated:** 16 November 2026  \n**Policy owner:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n> **Draft notice:** This policy is intended for Alpha beta builds and beta participation. It does not establish that a particular beta program, eligibility process, or feedback channel is currently active.\n\n## 1. Purpose\n\nBeta testing allows selected users to try pre-release Alpha software or features and report defects, compatibility issues, and usability feedback before a wider release.\n\nBeta software may be incomplete, unstable, or subject to change. Users should not rely on a beta build as if it were a final release.\n\n## 2. Eligibility and access\n\nAlpha must publish the eligibility criteria, application/selection process, and access duration for each beta program.\n\nThe Alpha rank **BETA** is a separately assignable beta-tester rank directly below **The Nibbler** in the project rank hierarchy. It must be labelled **BETA**. A BETA rank assignment does not, by itself, prove that a specific build or test environment is currently available.\n\n**Beta eligibility, assignment authority, and program access rules:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 3. Tester responsibilities\n\nTesters should:\n- use the build only for the stated test purpose;\n- read the build-specific notes and known issues;\n- report reproducible defects with relevant version and environment details;\n- distinguish observed behavior from assumptions;\n- avoid sharing credentials, tokens, or unnecessary personal data in reports;\n- follow the applicable Terms of Service, Acceptable Use Policy, and Community Guidelines.\n\nTesters must not:\n- exploit a test build to access accounts, data, or systems without authorization;\n- intentionally disrupt shared test infrastructure;\n- misrepresent a known defect as a confirmed security incident without evidence;\n- publish another person's private information or confidential test data.\n\n## 4. Stability, data loss, and compatibility\n\nA beta build may contain defects that cause crashes, performance problems, incompatibility, or loss/corruption of local or server-side test data. Back up important data where a safe backup option exists.\n\nAlpha must state known risks and any backup or reset behavior that testers need to understand before a test begins.\n\n**Build-specific backup, reset, and data-loss notice:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 5. Feedback and diagnostic information\n\nA beta report may include the information a tester chooses to submit, such as build version, operating system, hardware/software context, steps to reproduce, screenshots, logs, and a description of the issue.\n\nDo not assume that Alpha automatically collects all diagnostics. The actual telemetry and crash-reporting behavior must be disclosed in POL-002 and any applicable notice.\n\n**Feedback channel and diagnostic collection inventory:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 6. Confidentiality\n\nA beta program may include unreleased features or material that Alpha has expressly designated confidential. Any confidentiality obligation must be clearly presented and accepted before it applies. This policy does not silently impose a separate non-disclosure agreement.\n\n**Whether a separate NDA or restricted-disclosure program is used:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 7. Changes, withdrawal, and removal from a test\n\nAlpha may change or end a test program, replace a build, or remove access where necessary for security, stability, or program administration, subject to applicable law and any separate agreement.\n\nThe grounds, notice, and review process for removing a tester must be defined before the program is launched.\n\n**Tester removal and appeal procedure:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 8. Feedback rights\n\nTesters retain rights they have in their own submissions. Before Alpha reuses feedback, screenshots, or other submitted material outside the purpose of handling the report, Alpha must state the relevant permission or licence clearly.\n\n**Feedback licence and attribution rules:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 9. Security issues\n\nDo not publicly disclose an exploitable vulnerability before giving Alpha a reasonable opportunity to assess and address it. Use the official route in POL-009 once published. Do not conduct testing beyond the authorized scope.\n\n## 10. Related policies\n\n- POL-001 — Terms of Service\n- POL-002 — Privacy Policy\n- POL-003 — Acceptable Use Policy\n- POL-006 — Ranks and Permissions Policy\n- POL-009 — Security and Vulnerability Disclosure Policy\n- POL-010 — Service Availability and Third-Party Services Policy\n\n## Internal completion checklist — remove before publication\n\n- [ ] Define the active beta programs, eligible builds, and selection process.\n- [ ] Confirm how the BETA rank is assigned and what it actually unlocks.\n- [ ] Define backup/reset risks and known-issue disclosures.\n- [ ] Verify feedback channels and diagnostic collection.\n- [ ] Decide whether any separate confidentiality agreement is used.\n- [ ] Define tester removal and appeal handling.\n- [ ] Define feedback reuse permissions.\n- [ ] Remove unresolved markers and obtain legal/product approval.\n"},{"id":"POL-009","title":"Security and Vulnerability Disclosure","category":"Safety","version":"0.1-draft","markdown":"# Alpha Security and Vulnerability Disclosure Policy\n\n**Document ID:** POL-009  \n**Version:** 0.1-draft  \n**Status:** Draft — reporting channel and scope not yet approved  \n**Effective date:** Not effective until formally published  \n**Last updated:** 16 November 2026  \n**Security contact:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n> **Draft notice:** This document is not a safe-harbor promise or authorization to test Alpha systems. Do not begin scanning or exploitation unless Alpha has explicitly authorized the activity.\n\n## 1. Purpose\n\nAlpha wants suspected security weaknesses to be reported responsibly so they can be assessed and, where appropriate, corrected. This policy describes a proposed coordinated-disclosure process. It becomes operational only when Alpha confirms the contact route, scope, and response ownership.\n\n## 2. Systems in scope\n\nThe intended scope may include Alpha-operated:\n- Alpha Launcher and update infrastructure;\n- Alpha Client services and account integrations;\n- Alpha Account and community backend;\n- Alpha-operated APIs, storage, and service functions.\n\n**Exact in-scope domains, applications, environments, and excluded systems:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\nThird-party services, Minecraft servers operated by others, Microsoft systems, Supabase infrastructure outside Alpha's authorization, and other external systems are not automatically in scope. Do not test them without the relevant owner's permission.\n\n## 3. Reporting a vulnerability\n\nSend a report through the official security contact once Alpha publishes it.\n\nA useful report should include:\n- a concise description and affected component;\n- the potential security impact;\n- reproducible steps that do not expose real users or data;\n- relevant version, endpoint, or environment details;\n- screenshots or proof-of-concept material where safe and necessary;\n- your preferred contact method for follow-up.\n\nDo not include passwords, access tokens, private messages, or unnecessary personal data in a report.\n\n**Official security email or reporting portal:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 4. Testing restrictions\n\nUnless Alpha has given explicit written authorization, do not:\n- access, modify, delete, or exfiltrate another person's data;\n- use phishing, social engineering, credential theft, or physical attacks;\n- perform denial-of-service or resource-exhaustion testing;\n- persist access, install malware, or alter production systems;\n- enumerate or test unrelated third-party infrastructure;\n- publish exploit details while the issue remains unremediated.\n\nStop testing and report the issue if you encounter personal data, credentials, or access beyond your own account.\n\n## 5. Proposed coordinated-disclosure process\n\nThe intended process is:\n1. Alpha receives and acknowledges the report.\n2. Alpha assesses validity, severity, affected versions, and immediate risk.\n3. Alpha and the reporter coordinate reasonable follow-up questions and remediation.\n4. Alpha prepares and deploys an appropriate fix or mitigation.\n5. Alpha and the reporter agree on a disclosure date and the information that can safely be published.\n\n**Acknowledgement target, triage target, remediation updates, and disclosure timeline:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\nNo response-time or remediation deadline is promised until Alpha has the capacity and process to meet it.\n\n## 6. Good-faith handling\n\nAlpha should assess reports on their technical merits and avoid penalizing a person solely for a good-faith report that follows an explicitly authorized scope. This draft does not grant immunity from civil or criminal law, third-party terms, or actions outside an authorized scope.\n\n**Safe-harbor wording and authorized testing rules:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION AND LEGAL REVIEW]\n\n## 7. Personal data and confidentiality\n\nReports may contain personal data or sensitive security information. Alpha must restrict access to people who need it to investigate and must handle it under POL-002. Reporters should minimize the data they submit and should not publicly disclose other users' information.\n\n## 8. Public disclosure\n\nDo not publish details that would enable exploitation while users remain at risk. Coordinate disclosure with Alpha. If no response is received, any later disclosure decision must take account of user safety, applicable law, and the actual risk.\n\n**Disclosure coordination contact and escalation route:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 9. Changes and contact\n\nAlpha will publish the current version and effective date of this policy. Material changes to scope or reporting channels must be reflected in the published version.\n\n**Security contact:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]  \n**Operator:** [FULL LEGAL NAME — REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 10. Related policies\n\n- POL-002 — Privacy Policy\n- POL-003 — Acceptable Use Policy\n- POL-005 — Staff Code of Conduct\n- POL-008 — Beta Testing Policy\n- POL-010 — Service Availability and Third-Party Services Policy\n\n## Internal completion checklist — do not advertise this as an active disclosure program until complete\n\n- [ ] Confirm the exact scope and exclusions.\n- [ ] Create and monitor an official security reporting channel.\n- [ ] Assign an incident owner and backup contact.\n- [ ] Set realistic acknowledgement and update targets.\n- [ ] Approve safe-harbor language and legal scope.\n- [ ] Define sensitive-report storage and access restrictions.\n- [ ] Define coordinated disclosure and emergency escalation.\n- [ ] Remove unresolved markers and approve the final policy.\n"},{"id":"POL-010","title":"Service Availability and Third-Party Services","category":"Services","version":"0.1-draft","markdown":"# Alpha Service Availability and Third-Party Services Policy\n\n**Document ID:** POL-010  \n**Version:** 0.1-draft  \n**Status:** Draft — not approved for publication  \n**Effective date:** Not effective until formally published  \n**Last updated:** 16 November 2026  \n**Operator:** [FULL LEGAL NAME — REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n> **Draft notice:** This policy describes how availability and external dependencies should be communicated. It does not promise a service level, uptime percentage, support schedule, or uninterrupted operation.\n\n## 1. Scope\n\nThis policy is intended to cover Alpha-operated software and online features, including Alpha Launcher, Alpha Client services, Alpha Accounts, community features, and related backend functions.\n\nIt does not make Alpha responsible for independent Minecraft servers, Microsoft services, Mojang services, internet providers, or other third-party systems.\n\n## 2. Availability\n\nAlpha services may be affected by maintenance, software updates, outages, security incidents, network failures, infrastructure problems, or third-party interruptions.\n\nUnless Alpha publishes a separate service-level agreement, no specific uptime percentage or uninterrupted availability is promised. This statement does not exclude mandatory legal rights or remedies.\n\n**Support hours, status page, incident notice channel, and any service commitments:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 3. Maintenance and updates\n\nAlpha may need to update software, databases, or infrastructure to maintain security, compatibility, and functionality. Where practical, Alpha should provide advance notice of planned maintenance that materially affects online features.\n\nUrgent security or stability work may require shorter notice. Alpha should communicate material effects and restoration information when it is safe and practical to do so.\n\n**Maintenance notice channel and operational owner:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 4. Third-party services\n\nAlpha's inspected architecture uses Supabase for account/backend functions. The inspected Launcher source also includes Microsoft account-related functionality. The exact contracts, subprocessors, service roles, and complete data flows must be documented in POL-002 before publication.\n\nA third-party provider may change, limit, or discontinue its own service. Alpha should explain material effects on Alpha features where reasonably possible. Alpha does not control third-party availability or third-party terms.\n\n**Confirmed provider list and service roles:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 5. Service changes and discontinuation\n\nAlpha may develop, change, replace, or discontinue features. Before a material change, Alpha should consider user impact, account data, saved progress, digital entitlements, and any applicable contractual or consumer obligations.\n\nDo not promise that user data will always be exportable, preserved, or recoverable unless the relevant process has been verified.\n\n**Notice period and data/entitlement handling for discontinued features:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 6. Data and service recovery\n\nBackup frequency, recovery-point objectives, recovery-time objectives, and disaster-recovery arrangements have not been established by the inspection used for this draft.\n\n**Backup, restore, and disaster-recovery commitments:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\nDo not publish a recovery guarantee until the operational process has been tested and approved.\n\n## 7. Reporting an outage\n\nWhen reporting an issue, provide the affected Alpha feature, approximate time, product/build version, and a description of the observed behavior. Avoid sending passwords, authentication tokens, or unrelated personal data.\n\n**Official support route and status updates:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 8. Related policies\n\n- POL-001 — Terms of Service\n- POL-002 — Privacy Policy\n- POL-007 — Store and Digital Content Policy\n- POL-008 — Beta Testing Policy\n- POL-009 — Security and Vulnerability Disclosure Policy\n\n## Internal completion checklist — remove before publication\n\n- [ ] Confirm the services and providers covered.\n- [ ] Identify the official status/incident communication channel.\n- [ ] Confirm maintenance and support practices.\n- [ ] Verify provider roles and subprocessors in POL-002.\n- [ ] Define actual backup and recovery commitments, if any.\n- [ ] Define handling of saved data and digital entitlements on discontinuation.\n- [ ] Obtain legal/product review and remove unresolved markers.\n"},{"id":"POL-011","title":"Cookie and Tracking Notice","category":"Privacy","version":"0.1-draft","markdown":"# Alpha Cookie and Tracking Notice\n\n**Document ID:** POL-011  \n**Version:** 0.1-draft  \n**Status:** Draft — applicability and technical inventory not verified  \n**Effective date:** Not effective until formally published  \n**Last updated:** 16 November 2026  \n**Operator:** [FULL LEGAL NAME — REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n> **Draft notice:** The technical review identified Launcher local-storage use for certain settings and saved Microsoft-account display information. It did not complete an inventory of browser cookies, web storage, analytics, telemetry, crash reporting, advertising technologies, or all third-party SDKs. This document must not be presented as a complete tracking notice yet.\n\n## 1. Purpose and scope\n\nThis notice is intended to explain cookies and similar technologies used by Alpha-operated websites, Launcher components, and other Alpha services where applicable.\n\nA cookie or similar technology may store information on a device or access information already stored there. Legal requirements depend on the technology, purpose, and service context.\n\n## 2. What has been identified so far\n\nThe Launcher source inspected for the policy work uses local storage for certain launcher settings and saved Microsoft-account display information.\n\nThis is not, by itself, proof that Alpha uses advertising cookies or analytics tracking. The inspection did not establish the complete inventory of:\n- browser cookies;\n- local storage and session storage keys;\n- embedded webview storage;\n- analytics or product-usage events;\n- crash-reporting and diagnostic SDKs;\n- advertising identifiers or cross-site tracking;\n- third-party scripts, pixels, or SDKs.\n\n**Complete technical inventory:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 3. Categories and purposes\n\nThe final notice must list each actual technology, its provider, purpose, data accessed or stored, duration, and whether it is strictly necessary or optional.\n\n| Category | Intended description | Current status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Strictly necessary storage | Authentication, security, session continuity, or a feature explicitly requested by the user | Exact keys/providers and legal assessment not verified |\n| Preference storage | Remembering user-selected settings | Launcher local settings storage identified; exact inventory required |\n| Analytics | Measuring usage or performance | Not established by the inspection |\n| Diagnostics | Crash reports and technical error information | Not established by the inspection |\n| Advertising/tracking | Advertising measurement or cross-service tracking | Not established by the inspection |\n\nThis table is a checklist of categories to assess, not a statement that every category is currently used.\n\n## 4. Consent and user controls\n\nWhere consent is legally required, optional technologies must not be activated before valid consent. Users must be able to refuse or withdraw consent as easily as they can give it. Strictly necessary technologies must be limited to what is required for the requested service.\n\n**Consent mechanism, preference controls, and withdrawal route:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\nDo not add a consent banner or claim consent is collected until the actual implementation and configuration have been checked.\n\n## 5. Third-party technologies\n\nIf a third party supplies analytics, diagnostics, embedded content, or other technologies, Alpha must identify the provider, explain its role, and link to the relevant privacy information. Any personal-data processing must also be reflected in POL-002.\n\n**Provider and transfer inventory:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 6. How to manage stored information\n\nThe final notice should explain how users can inspect, clear, or control relevant storage in the Launcher, browser, or operating system, including any effect on saved settings or sign-in state.\n\n**Verified user instructions for each supported platform:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 7. Changes and contact\n\nThe published notice will show its version and effective date. Material changes to tracking technologies or purposes must be reflected in the notice and consent mechanism where required.\n\n**Privacy contact:** [REQUIRES ALPHA CONFIRMATION]\n\n## 8. Related policies\n\n- POL-002 — Privacy Policy\n- POL-001 — Terms of Service\n\n## Internal technical completion checklist — remove before publication\n\n- [ ] Inspect the full Launcher code and packaged dependencies for cookies, web storage, SDKs, analytics, diagnostics, and embedded webviews.\n- [ ] Inspect Alpha Client and any Alpha-operated websites for equivalent technologies.\n- [ ] Inspect production configuration and network behavior; source search alone is insufficient.\n- [ ] Record each provider, purpose, key, duration, and data category.\n- [ ] Determine which technologies require consent under Dutch/EU rules.\n- [ ] Verify consent/refusal/withdrawal behavior and test that optional technologies remain off until consent.\n- [ ] Update POL-002 for any personal-data processing and international transfers.\n- [ ] Obtain legal review and remove unresolved markers before publication.\n"}];

function escapeHtml(value) {
  return String(value).replace(/[&<>"']/g, char => ({"&":"&amp;","<":"&lt;",">":"&gt;",'"':"&quot;","'":"&#39;"}[char]));
}
function inlineMarkdown(text) {
  let value = escapeHtml(text);
  value = value.replace(/\*\*(.+?)\*\*/g,"<strong>$1</strong>");
  value = value.replace(/\*(.+?)\*/g,"<em>$1</em>");
  value = value.replace(/\x60([^\x60]+)\x60/g,"<code>$1</code>");
  value = value.replace(/\[([^\]]+)\]\((https?:\/\/[^\s)]+)\)/g,'<a href="$2" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">$1</a>');
  return value;
}
function renderMarkdown(markdown) {
  const lines = markdown.replace(/\r/g,"").split("\n");
  const output = [];
  let paragraph = [], listType = null, tableRows = [], quote = [];
  const flushParagraph = () => { if(paragraph.length){output.push("<p>"+inlineMarkdown(paragraph.join(" "))+"</p>");paragraph=[];} };
  const flushList = () => { if(listType){output.push("</"+listType+">");listType=null;} };
  const flushQuote = () => { if(quote.length){output.push("<blockquote>"+quote.map(line=>"<p>"+inlineMarkdown(line)+"</p>").join("")+"</blockquote>");quote=[];} };
  const flushTable = () => {
    if(!tableRows.length)return;
    const rows = tableRows.filter(row=>!/^\s*\|?\s*:?-{3,}/.test(row));
    output.push("<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>"+rows.map((row,index)=>{
      const cells=row.trim().replace(/^\||\|$/g,"").split("|").map(cell=>cell.trim());
      const tag=index===0?"th":"td";
      return "<tr>"+cells.map(cell=>"<"+tag+">"+inlineMarkdown(cell)+"</"+tag+">").join("")+"</tr>";
    }).join("")+"</table></div>");
    tableRows=[];
  };
  for(const raw of lines){
    const line=raw.trim();
    if(!line){flushParagraph();flushList();flushQuote();flushTable();continue;}
    if(line.startsWith("|")){flushParagraph();flushList();flushQuote();tableRows.push(line);continue;}
    flushTable();
    if(line.startsWith(">")){flushParagraph();flushList();quote.push(line.replace(/^>\s?/,""));continue;}
    flushQuote();
    const heading=line.match(/^(#{1,4})\s+(.+)$/);
    if(heading){flushParagraph();flushList();const level=heading[1].length;output.push("<h"+level+">"+inlineMarkdown(heading[2])+"</h"+level+">");continue;}
    if(/^---+$/.test(line)){flushParagraph();flushList();output.push("<hr>");continue;}
    const unordered=line.match(/^[-*]\s+(.+)$/);
    const ordered=line.match(/^\d+\.\s+(.+)$/);
    if(unordered||ordered){flushParagraph();const type=unordered?"ul":"ol";if(listType!==type){flushList();listType=type;output.push("<"+type+">");}output.push("<li>"+inlineMarkdown((unordered||ordered)[1])+"</li>");continue;}
    flushList();paragraph.push(line);
  }
  flushParagraph();flushList();flushQuote();flushTable();
  return output.join("");
}
const root=document.getElementById("policy-documents");
root.innerHTML=policies.map(policy=>'<section class="policy-document" id="'+policy.id.toLowerCase()+'">'+
  '<div class="policy-document-heading"><span class="policy-document-number">'+policy.id+'</span><span class="policy-document-category">'+escapeHtml(policy.category)+'</span><span class="policy-document-status">DRAFT · NOT EFFECTIVE</span></div>'+
  '<h2>'+escapeHtml(policy.title)+'</h2>'+
  '<div class="policy-document-version">Version '+escapeHtml(policy.version)+' · Working draft</div>'+
  '<article class="markdown-body">'+renderMarkdown(policy.markdown)+'</article>'+
'</section>').join("");
document.getElementById("policy-count").textContent=String(policies.length).padStart(2,"0");
